<$fill $name="body">

!! What it does

Drop a binary file (PDF, image, office document, archive, …) onto a tiddler in the story, or onto a stream row, and it becomes a tiddler right away: the file is stored in the wiki as base64, no `$:/Import` dialog, no server needed. A line that shows the attachment is appended to the end of the tiddler you dropped on, a transclusion of the new tiddler. It is ordinary text, so keep, move or delete it. Anything else is up to your wiki's configuration: link the new tiddler to the one you dropped on, give it fields.

Pasting works too: click a tiddler to select it (a thin bar on its left edge marks it), then paste a file or a screenshot.

!! How you use it

# Drag a file from your file manager onto a tiddler. The tiddler gets a dashed outline; release.
# The new tiddler is called `<tiddler>/<file name>` (configurable). A notice says what was attached.
# To paste: click the tiddler, then Ctrl+V. Without a selected tiddler a notice tells you to select one.
# Text files (`.md`, `.json`, `.tid`, `.csv`, …) are not attached: they go to TiddlyWiki's normal import, as before. Dropping outside any tiddler also behaves as before.

!! A new version of a file

Drop //one// file onto a tiddler that is itself a binary (an image, a PDF, … — embedded as base64, or with `_canonical_uri`). The plugin asks "Replace the content of <title> with <file name>?"; on OK the file becomes that tiddler's content: base64 text and the file's type (which may change, e.g. png → jpg). Title, tags, caption, created and all other fields stay; `modified` is updated. Nothing is attached and no line is appended. System tiddlers and text tiddlers are never replaced, and several files dropped at once are attached as usual.

The new version is always embedded, so a `_canonical_uri` is removed. If it pointed into `files/` (TW Desk's folder), the tiddler gets `tw-desk-update-of: files/<name>`: when the wiki stops, TW Desk writes the new version over that file instead of creating a second one. The plugin itself never writes into `files/`.

!! What the plugin does not decide

The plugin ships //no// linking. Linking to the drop target, extra fields, a kind type — all of that is a small action tiddler in your wiki (see //Examples//). The plugin provides the variables and runs your actions.

!! Getting the files out later

Base64 makes a single-file wiki big. A command-line run turns the attachments into external files plus `_canonical_uri` whenever you want (see //Reference → Extracting files//). On a Node.js server wiki TiddlyWiki already saves binary tiddlers as real files on disk.

